1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:08,064
If your house runs on propane, heating oil or electric baseboards,
someone has probably told you a heat pump is the easy win.

2
00:00:08,067 --> 00:00:13,947
But does a heat pump always beat each of them on
running cost, or does one of them break the rule?

3
00:00:14,967 --> 00:00:25,743
The home-energy marketplace EnergySage puts it flatly: "Heat pumps consistently
save against propane, oil, and electric resistance heating almost everywhere."

4
00:00:25,767 --> 00:00:35,991
Abode Energy Management, in a video on heat pump economics in Massachusetts, says
home heating oil is "always also going to be more expensive than a heat pump".

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:47,904
Yet The Boston Globe reported that in a winter of cheaper oil, even Massachusetts homeowners switching
from oil "could see their energy costs increase by a few hundred dollars over the cold season".

6
00:00:48,933 --> 00:00:51,957
So who is right? Start
with the widest view.

7
00:00:51,967 --> 00:01:06,223
A national study estimated that among homes heating mainly with electricity, fuel oil or propane that already
have air conditioning, 95 to 100 percent would see lower bills with a heat pump, depending on its efficiency.

8
00:01:06,533 --> 00:01:11,861
The median saving it estimated
was $300 to $650 a year.

9
00:01:11,867 --> 00:01:25,163
Homes on those fuels without air conditioning did worse: only 73 to 86 percent would come out ahead, and most of
the homes whose bills went up were in hotter climates, where the new cooling cost more than the heating saved.

10
00:01:25,167 --> 00:01:35,991
For comparison, among gas-heated homes with air conditioning, the same study put
the median anywhere from a $70 loss to a $380 saving, depending on the heat pump.

11
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,912
That was an estimate. What about
heat pumps running in real homes?

12
00:01:40,933 --> 00:01:52,429
A study of cold-climate heat pumps found the all-electric systems cut heating costs by 53 percent
on average, measured against what electric resistance heaters would have used for the same heat.

13
00:01:52,433 --> 00:02:03,809
An evaluation of ductless mini-split heat pumps found them more cost-effective than electric
resistance and propane heat at every energy price and every outdoor temperature it examined.

14
00:02:03,833 --> 00:02:11,081
Oil was different: there, the relative price of oil decided
the outdoor temperature above which the heat pump won.

15
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:26,052
So the evaluators advised oil-heated homes to switch the heat pump off when it got very cold, when the oil system would run
cheaper: below 8 degrees Fahrenheit in one year, and below 25 degrees the next, depending on that year's energy prices.

16
00:02:27,067 --> 00:02:41,875
And another study found that in regions where homes heat with electricity or fuels other than gas, heating is generally more
expensive and the net benefits of a heat pump are higher, though climate and energy prices also play an important role.

17
00:02:41,900 --> 00:03:01,508
Real projects show the same split: across 273 home electrification projects with cost data, some raised annual energy costs, in some cases by as much
as $1,000 a year, while the big reductions were most likely in homes with high bills beforehand, such as those heating with propane or fuel oil.

18
00:03:01,533 --> 00:03:03,909
So does a heat pump always win?

19
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:11,557
Against propane and electric resistance heat, this
evidence says it nearly always does on running cost.

20
00:03:11,867 --> 00:03:18,059
Against oil, not always: whether it wins depends
on the price of oil and how cold it gets.

21
00:03:18,067 --> 00:03:25,387
What none of this can do is price your own house: the results
moved with air conditioning, climate and energy prices.

22
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:32,856
If you heat with propane or baseboards, get installed quotes
and set them against what you spend on fuel each winter.

23
00:03:32,867 --> 00:03:44,291
If you heat with oil, ask a different question: at this winter's oil and electricity prices, below
what outdoor temperature does your old system run cheaper, and will you keep it for those days?

24
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:47,996
Check your fuel and your rates
before you check the brochure.
